**Paging**: page = address divided by page size (whole part); offset = remainder; look up the frame; physical = frame x page size + offset. If the table says the page is not in memory, a page fault occurs.

**Segmentation**: read (segment, offset); if offset >= limit, report an addressing error; otherwise physical = base + offset.

**Bits**: a logical address of n bits with pages of $2^{k}$ bytes has k offset bits and n - k bits for the page number, so up to $2^{n-k}$ pages.

```c
/* Example of logic: paging with 1024-byte pages (not compiled; checked by a Python translation). */
int translate(int logical, int page_table[])
{
    int page   = logical / 1024;
    int offset = logical % 1024;
    int frame  = page_table[page];
    return frame * 1024 + offset;
}
```
With the page table `{5, 2, 9, 7, 0}`, `translate(3500, ...)` gives 7596.
